WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.851
Film Courage: I think there’s a lot of social
pressure and I know for Generation Xers like

00:00:03.851 --> 00:00:10.090
myself it’s always existed but now for Millennials
and Generation Z this…not even just the

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:17.490
30 Under 30 of people [who are successful],
now there is 25 under 25.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.303
We see younger and younger people achieving
great things.

00:00:19.303 --> 00:00:24.430
Jeffrey Davis: What I tell my students is
great, get into the business in any job you

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:30.550
can get into, pay your dues, and don’t say
“Oh within a year I’ve got to be in a

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.220
writer’s room as an assistant.”

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.560
Because 9 chances out of 10, it’s not going
to happen.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.120
If the lucky chance that it happens, great
for you!

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.930
And then bring your friends along.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:46.219
Because we tell (particularly grad students)
on the first day of grad school look right

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:50.200
and look left because that’s not just your
posse, those are the people that are going

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.289
to hire you.

00:00:54.289 --> 00:00:59.600
But LMU [Loyola Marymount University] is not
like other film schools, it’s not competitive,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.360
it’s more collaborative.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:09.689
I know USC is very competitive and I think
that’s too bad because collaborative environment

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:14.619
is always better to nurture other people while
you’re trying to get what you want, that

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:16.439
means that you will always have people that
you’re working with.

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:21.180
Most of my seniors come out and if they haven’t
changed their mind about writing, they work.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.420
They are all listed.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:26.060
This crop of seniors that just graduated in
May, they’re all working in the business.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.639
Peter Desberg: But we will want to edit out
all of the bad stuff about USC out of there.

00:01:29.639 --> 00:01:30.639
Jeffrey Davis: Nahhh!

00:01:30.639 --> 00:01:31.639
Keep it in.

00:01:31.639 --> 00:01:32.799
Peter Desberg: [Raising his hand] USC graduate
here.

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:38.069
Film Courage: Well, we can ask this thought
(no schools mentioned though), how can one

00:01:38.069 --> 00:01:41.090
tell a collaborative environment versus competitive.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:46.020
Because sometimes competitive can mask as
collaborative and then once you’ve been

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.369
around a little bit [in this environment]
you start to see “No…”

00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:54.700
Jeffrey Davis: They care not just about their
own work but other people’s work, are they

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:55.700
involved?

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.099
Do they work on other people’s work?

00:01:58.099 --> 00:02:01.139
I think that’s a big tell.

00:02:01.139 --> 00:02:03.819
And what are the teachers like?

00:02:03.819 --> 00:02:06.079
What are your bosses like?

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:10.560
Now the difference between school and a job.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.599
If your boss is not a nice person, there’s
not much you can do about that except quit.

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:23.080
And it’s good for you to work for mean people
(I think) because you will learn especially

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.060
early on, you will learn how to deal with
difficult people.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.950
It’s just a part of life.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.030
Look, I have to work with Peter everyday.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.370
Peter Desberg: But you’ve asked a really
nuanced question.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.629
Jeffrey Davis: Which is another pedagogical
term - nuanced.

00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:40.250
Film Courage: Oh good!

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:41.250
I’m “nuanced.”

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.969
Peter Desberg: Because you’re making it
sound like it’s binary.

00:02:44.969 --> 00:02:45.969
Are you collaborative…

00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:48.609
Jeffrey Davis: We don’t know the word binary,
Peter.

00:02:48.609 --> 00:02:50.349
Film Courage: 0, 0, 1…

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:52.099
Jeffrey Davis: She’s thinking mathematically.

00:02:52.099 --> 00:02:54.049
He means it’s not one or the other.

00:02:54.049 --> 00:02:55.469
Peter Desberg: I’ll get my answer in.

00:02:55.469 --> 00:02:58.950
I’m patient just like Adam.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.780
Jeffrey Davis: If you start speaking English,
perhaps?

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:11.180
Peter Desberg: And so the fact is in any environment
the difference, the places where you find

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:17.510
it’s competitive, you’ll find people being
very evaluative, particularly negatively evaluative.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.110
“That’s not a good idea because…”

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.110
“That wouldn’t work because…”

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:22.010
Jeffrey Davis: I don’t allow that in my
class.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:27.659
Peter Desberg: But good ideas are always recognized.

00:03:27.659 --> 00:03:31.989
So it’s always competitive because if you
have the best ideas, you’re recognized as

00:03:31.989 --> 00:03:33.499
the best.

00:03:33.499 --> 00:03:41.439
Like LMU works really hard to be a supportive
environment, yet here was this student who

00:03:41.439 --> 00:03:44.530
was clearly head and shoulder above everybody
else.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:47.739
You’re going to get noticed by the quality
of your ideas.

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:52.519
So it’s competitive to the extent that you
could stratify who are the best of the worst

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:58.080
writers whether you’re supportive or competitive.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.440
But the difference is the lumps you take along
the way.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.980
In other words some people again come with
this idea of “Look it’s an aggressive

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:07.980
marketplace.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.590
We want to prepare you for it.”

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.980
So if your ideas aren’t so great, we’re
going to let you know it because you won’t

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:17.000
have so many not-so-great ideas later as opposed
to other places.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:23.320
I mean, I know for example people come to
me all the time with ideas saying “Can you

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.440
give me notes?

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.580
Here’s my script, etc.”

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:27.580
And what I found…

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.199
Jeffrey Davis: You should charge for that
I think.

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:35.600
Peter Desberg: What I find is I almost never
give criticisms.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.660
What I do is I ask questions and so if something
doesn’t work for me, I don’t say “That

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:41.660
doesn’t work.”

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:42.660
or “That’s no good.”

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.930
I say “Have you thought about this?”

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:49.050
Or “Why is it work to have this be that?

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.340
Or is there another way to do that?”

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.660
So it’s never coming out as a put-down because
people get very defensive when you put their

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:56.660
stuff down.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.180
Jeffrey Davis: It doesn’t help them either.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:04.050
Peter Desberg: And I don’t want to dampen
their enthusiasm and at the same point I don’t

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:05.590
think it’s helpful.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:09.900
We’re asking them thought-provoking questions
that they can answer.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:15.220
Eventually if they are smart enough, it’s
a criticism or it’s a flaw, but it’s not

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.720
negative, it makes them think “How do I
solve a problem?”

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.310
So I like to re-cast it as problem-solving.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:26.610
Jeffrey Davis: You know I think it helps to
realize that believe it or not Aaron Sorkin

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:32.410
has written some pretty crummy scripts and
he would probably be the first to say so event

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:44.080
he best people…well there’s a famous William
Somerset Maugham saying that only mediocre

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.050
writers are at their best all the time.

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:56.230
So I think what I try to do in class and probably
some of this is influenced by Peter is that

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:59.630
(it really hurts to have to compliment him,
physically hurts).

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.700
Peter Desberg: You can see the pain in his
face.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.460
You won’t see me kicking his ankle.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:08.930
Jeffrey Davis: It would have never occurred
to me to say to any student and I haven’t

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:11.740
had the experience of a student with zero
talent.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:18.940
I’ve never had that because it’s really
hard to get into LMU now and we are number

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:25.550
8 out of 10 top film schools now, so plug
for LMU (Loyola Marymount University).

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:33.699
But even someone who was really untalented
I would probably do what Peter just said I

00:06:33.699 --> 00:06:38.389
would probably say do you really want to write
but I would never say “You have no talent

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:39.389
for writing.”

00:06:39.389 --> 00:06:42.930
Because a lot of people said that to me, they
didn’t say it in those words but a lot of

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:48.770
people discouraged me and I think discouraging
someone is really awful, it’s an awful thing

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:53.979
to do because you never know what someone
will do later.

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:56.479
Peter Desberg: But let me add…

00:06:56.479 --> 00:06:57.750
Jeffrey Davis: Hold on…hold on…stay.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.419
Peter Desberg: I almost got a word in.

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:03.580
Jeffrey Davis: I wasn’t finished with my
speech, I was going to say something nice

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:04.750
about you.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:10.150
If you had told me 10 years ago when I met
Peter that he was going to write a novel,

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:12.909
I would have said “Peter will never write
a novel.

00:07:12.909 --> 00:07:14.680
That’s something Peter doesn’t do.”

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:17.520
He’s not only written a novel, he’s really
working hard on it.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.949
Now you can interrupt me, go ahead.

00:07:18.949 --> 00:07:21.210
Peter Desberg: Notice he didn’t say “It’s
really good.”

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:22.220
He said “He’s really working hard.”

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:23.220
Jeffrey Davis: It’s getting there.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:25.310
Film Courage: Is that the manual?

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:26.380
Peter Desberg: No.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:32.220
Jeffrey Davis: It’s a historical novel which
by the way is the hardest genre there is.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:38.340
Peter Desberg: But you know even when you’re
asking questions, I think you have to be really

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:43.590
careful because questions can be very evaluative.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:46.370
“Why did you decide to wear that dress today?”

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.090
Jeffrey Davis: It’s a very pretty dress.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:49.090
Film Courage: It’s hot outside.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.300
Peter Desberg: The point is that’s a question…

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.580
Film Courage: Oh yeah…I’ve had those type
of questions.

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.409
Peter Desberg: It’s an evaluation.

00:07:55.409 --> 00:07:57.599
Jeffrey Davis: That’s a gender-biased question.

00:07:57.599 --> 00:08:01.889
Peter Desberg: Even asking questions “Why
do you want to be writing?”

00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:06.300
You have to be very careful because that could
be a very discouraging question.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.219
Film Courage: Well I have an evaluative question
that I would like to ask and that would be

00:08:11.219 --> 00:08:15.850
do you think that a writer that has talent
but no connections has an easier time getting

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:22.539
in the business versus a writer that has not
as much talent but connections?

00:08:22.539 --> 00:08:25.509
Peter Desberg: That’s a trick question.

00:08:25.509 --> 00:08:26.750
Film Courage: It’s not evaluative?

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:27.750
Jeffrey Davis: You’re sneaky.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:32.100
Peter Desberg: Because it really depends on
the ratios of both.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:34.780
Jeffrey Davis: It depends on the circumstances,
thats the scientific answer.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:41.750
Peter Desberg: But I have to tell you contacts…oh
it’s really big!

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:45.820
You know there are an awful lot of people
who will never, never, never get noticed no

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:50.980
matter how good they are because the work
will just never get out of that bottle.

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:55.320
And knowing people, big advantage.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:00.530
But you know we were talking about this before
today that the importance of the M word in

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:04.500
Hollywood, that networking is more important
than anything.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:11.700
The more people you can work with, the more
people you can collaborate with, when there’s

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:17.850
a position open in a room you’re a lot more
likely to call friends in than say “Hey,

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.160
we got an open spot here.

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:25.560
Come in and audition” So more work gets
given to people who know people than any other

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:26.560
way.

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:29.730
Film Courage: I like what you said about the
evaluative questions though because I think

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:35.730
we’ve all had that happen to us in one form
or another where somebody’s asking a question

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.600
and they’re like “I’m just asking a
question!”

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.100
But it’s the intention behind the question.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:45.680
Peter Desberg: Well I had a clinical supervisor
who would really get down on me with “Don’t

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.060
make a statement through a question.”

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:54.660
Film Courage: I’m almost afraid to follow
up with something.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:10:00.720
Peter Desberg: If I said we’ll use the same
question again “Um…why did you choose

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.790
to wear that dress today?”

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:06.100
That’s like saying “That’s not really
an appropriate dress to wear?”

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:07.270
Film Courage: Oh…I can tell the intention…yeah.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:15.070
Peter Desberg: And so again, you have to be
careful even when you think, “Why does your

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:19.400
character, why did he choose to be a cowboy?”

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:23.470
You know, I’m saying “You shouldn’t
of had him be a cowboy.”

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.970
Film Courage: I can see it in your face and
intonation.

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:30.240
Jeffrey Davis: If you are paying for it, I
think that changes the situation.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.910
Peter Desberg: I think the important thing
is when you’re asking questions, to give

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:41.490
the appearance of wanting information rather
than giving an evaluation.

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:44.830
And surprisingly people don’t really like
being evaluated negatively that much.

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:45.830
Film Courage: That is surprising.

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:49.750
Peter Desberg: No matter how much practice
they’ve had.

